## 2. The Deck-Action No-Go

Let

\[
 p:C^\circ\longrightarrow B^\circ
\]

be the selected degree-three cover restricted to the complement of its branch
locus.  The pinned q79 theorem gives

\[
 \operatorname{im}\mu=S_3,
 \qquad
 \mu:\pi_1(B^\circ,b)\longrightarrow S_3.
\]

For a connected cover, its deck group is the centralizer of the monodromy
image in the permutation group of a fiber.  Here

\[
 \operatorname{Deck}(C^\circ/B^\circ)
 \cong C_{S_3}(S_3)=Z(S_3)=\{1\}.
\]

The certificate checks all six permutation matrices and finds exactly one
centralizing permutation.  Hence the q79 cover is not an `S3`-Galois cover.
There is no nontrivial global `S3` deck action to lift.

This does not remove `S3`.  It says that `S3` is holonomy, not a global sheet
symmetry.  A global sheet labelling is neither available nor required.

## 3. The Associated Strain Local System

Let

\[
 W=\mathbb R^3_D\oplus\mathbb R^3_E
\]

carry the two-copy permutation representation

\[
 \rho_W(\sigma)=P_\sigma\oplus P_\sigma.
\]

The monodromy homomorphism defines the global flat associated bundle

\[
 \mathcal W
 =\widetilde B^\circ\times_{\rho_W\circ\mu}W.
\]

This bundle is globally defined even though no global ordering of its three
sheets exists.  The associated strain local system therefore replaces a
globally labelled sheet carrier.  Its Reynolds endomorphism is

\[
 P_{\rm R}
 =\frac1{6}\sum_{\sigma\in S_3}\rho_W(\sigma).
\]

For every \(\tau\in S_3\),

\[
 \rho_W(\tau)P_{\rm R}\rho_W(\tau)^{-1}=P_{\rm R}.
\]

Therefore `P_R` is independent of a local sheet frame and descends to a
parallel global endomorphism of \(\mathcal W\).  So does

\[
 Q_{\rm TT}=I-P_{\rm R}.
\]

The exact ranks are

\[
 \operatorname{rank}P_{\rm R}=2,
 \qquad
 \operatorname{rank}Q_{\rm TT}=4.
\]

Thus the two trivial modes and the four-dimensional TT complement are global
subbundles on the branch complement.  This is the correct global meaning of
the earlier finite `S3` Reynolds split.

