## 5. Binary-Monodromy Reynolds Descent

Write

\[
 \pi:\operatorname{Dic}_3\longrightarrow S_3
\]

for the selected two-to-one binary projection.  The strain action factors
through \(\pi\):

\[
 \widetilde\rho_W(g)=\rho_W(\pi(g)).
\]

In particular the central element \(-1\in\operatorname{Dic}_3\) acts as
`+I` on `W`.  Every \(\sigma\in S_3\) has exactly two preimages, and therefore

\[
 \begin{aligned}
 \frac1{12}\sum_{g\in\operatorname{Dic}_3}\widetilde\rho_W(g)
 &=\frac1{12}\sum_{\sigma\in S_3}
   \sum_{g\in\pi^{-1}(\sigma)}\rho_W(\sigma)\\
 &=\frac1{6}\sum_{\sigma\in S_3}\rho_W(\sigma)\\
 &=P_{\rm R}.
 \end{aligned}
\]

This is an exact equality of rational `6 x 6` matrices in the certificate.
Consequently a change of local binary lift, \(g\mapsto-g\), cannot change the
strain Reynolds projector.

The unsolved global `w2` or SpinC decision still matters for genuine spinor
carriers.  It does not obstruct the vector or quadratic-strain Reynolds
descent proved here.  No global Spin lift is claimed.

## 6. Operator Naturality Implies Green Naturality

Let \(L\) be the selected gauge-fixed Hessian once it is constructed on
\(\mathcal W\), and let \(T_\gamma\) be holonomy around a loop.  Assume

1. the domain of \(L\) and its harmonic complement are invariant under
   \(T_\gamma\);
2. \(L T_\gamma=T_\gamma L\); and
3. \(G_Q\) is the inverse of \(L\) on that complement.

Then both \(T_\gamma G_QT_\gamma^{-1}\) and \(G_Q\) are inverses of \(L\) on
the same complement.  Uniqueness gives

\[
 T_\gamma G_QT_\gamma^{-1}=G_Q.
\]

Thus Green equivariance is not a second symmetry axiom.  It follows from the
global naturality of the selected operator and its domain.  The certificate
contains the exact witness

\[
 L_0=2P_{\rm R}+3Q_{\rm TT},
 \qquad
 G_0=\tfrac12P_{\rm R}+\tfrac13Q_{\rm TT},
 \qquad
 L_0G_0=G_0L_0=I,
\]

and verifies commutation with every monodromy matrix.  This witness proves the
finite algebra only; it is not the physical q79 HYM Hessian.

## 7. Why C4 Remains Independent

On `W`, the target quarter-turn is

\[
 J_{DE}(d,e)=(-e,d),
 \qquad
 J_{DE}^2=-I,
 \qquad
 J_{DE}^4=I.
\]

Since the two lanes carry equal `S3` representations,

\[
 [J_{DE},\rho_W(\sigma)]=0
 \quad\text{for every }\sigma\in S_3.
\]

The target `S3` holonomy and `C4` action are therefore compatible.  But the
binary central element acts as `+I` on strain, whereas
\(J_{DE}^2=-I\).  The `Dic3` center cannot be identified with the square of
the missing quarter-turn on this carrier.  A central-product shortcut is
therefore excluded at the strain level.

What remains is one independent source-side `C4` lift that

```text
